#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133398 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/id #uuid "bb373a5d-aa71-58ed-82e3-b74a8f51d144" :question/math-academy-id "e-114" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2$. According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2150/Source/Images/0d850b5400cf74888676e94e8c489eff.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6]$, so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nhas a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6)$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(6)=11\n$$\n\n, we must have\n$k\\in[3,11]$.\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function,\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nmay have solutions for $k < 3$, but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133399 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d048aae2-5773-5da7-ab18-35d41bfbb53a" :question/math-academy-id "e-115" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2]$. Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2]$.\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$. Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}]$, the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$. Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}]$, the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$. Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}]$, the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}$. Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}]$, the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1]$. Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/id #uuid "89750383-d5a6-5bf4-b6b2-abd489e44d3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-116" :question/problem "On which type of closed intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\,$?" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0$, which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1$.\n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1$.\n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "e32fb82c-2e8f-5383-b15a-8f98aa86b716" :question/math-academy-id "e-6753" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2]$. By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2150/Source/Images/6e256c36411942eac5fc5523f999c4c3.png)\n\n> *If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{________}$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k$.*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2]$, so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nhas a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2)$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-5)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(2)=4\n$$\n\n, we must have $k\\in[1,4]$.\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n> *If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82bf6727-4d46-53cc-bd94-e1fb9aeb88de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132813} {:answer/id #uuid "88b6380f-e14b-55cd-8abc-53e091d1b0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132814} {:answer/id #uuid "b125e1ed-834c-589a-98d8-1461d2f86062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132815} {:answer/id #uuid "ec348464-4f61-5199-a95b-bcd9353d2446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132816} {:answer/id #uuid "65ed2b30-a74a-573b-911b-be1e328a7d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b125e1ed-834c-589a-98d8-1461d2f86062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132815} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c4077f6-7b12-53f5-bb48-f706b93e197a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132812}] :question/id #uuid "5f019924-a46f-5bc3-be22-35c5680cdf03" :question/math-academy-id "q-47058" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = (x + 1)^{2} - 4$. For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x) = k$ has a solution on the interval $x ∈ [- 4,-2]$?"}]}
